'''''Eryngium amethystinum''''', the '''amethyst eryngo''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=ERAM11|taxon=Eryngium amethystinum|accessdate=19 January 2016}}</ref> '''Italian eryngo'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> or '''amethyst sea holly''', is a clump-forming, [[Perennial plant|perennial]], tap-rooted [[herb]]. Its [[Plant stem|stem]] is {{Convert abbreviated|30 to 50|cm}} long and is light blue to purple in colour. It has a basal circle of [[Leaf shape|obovate]], [[Leaf shape|pinnate]], spiny, leathery, mid-green leaves. It flowers in mid to late summer with cylindrical [[umbel]]s, 2–3&nbsp;cm long atop silvery blue bracts and branching stems. The plant is native to the eastern [[Mediterranean]] and prefers dry places and [[soil]]s that are rich in [[calcium]].<ref>The Royal Horticultural Society Encyclopedia of Garden Plants, Ed. Christopher Brickell. Dorling Kindersley Ltd. London 1996. p414 {{ISBN|0 7513 0436 0}}</ref>

